name: Getting Started
order: 102
---------------------

Thank you for <a href="#.install">installing</a> _Collider.JAM_!

Now you can create your first _Collider.JAM_ project.
The simplest one is called a *mod*.
We are not diving into details here, but naming and structure are super-important in Collider.JAM, so at the start, follow the suggested naming to the letter.

Let's visualize a bouncing planet.

Create a new folder named _planet.mod_ - this is going
to be a home of our Collider.JAM project.
The ".mod" extension is crucial, since that is how the framework
detects the root of the project.

First, we need to find a suitable picture of a planet.

You can get one at
[GitHub](https://github.com/invadium/procedural-november.pak/tree/main/planets), or from
[Open Game Art](https://opengameart.org/content/mars-type-planet),
or find any other planet image with a transparent background.

Download the image, rename it to _planet.png_ 
or _planet.jpeg_/_planet.jpg_ if that is an JPEG image),
and place it in the folder named _res_ inside your _planet.mod_.

That folder is going to hold our assets.

Now, create a file _planet.mod/lab.js_:

```
    // ===================
    // planet.mod/lab.js
    // ===================

    // position at the center of the screen
    let x = rx(.5) // place at 50% of the screen width
    let y = ry(.5)  // place at 50% of the screen height
    let a = 0 // planet angle in radians
    let r = 50 // planet radius in pixels

    let dx = 100   // x-axis speed component
    let dy = 100   // y-axis speed component

    function init() {
        this.background = '#000000' // black color in hex RGB
    }

    function evo(dt) {
       // move the planet coordinates factored by the provided delta time dt
       x += dx * dt
       y += dy * dt
       // rotate the planet 45 degrees per second counterclockwise
       a -= .25 * PI * dt

       // bounce from the screen edges
       if (x > rx(1)-r && dx > 0) dx *= -1
       else if (x < r && dx < 0) dx *= -1
       if (y > ry(1)-r && dy > 0) dy *= -1
       else if (y < r && dy < 0) dy *= -1
    }

    function draw() {
        // we MUST save the drawing context before any transformation
        // and then translate to the planet coordinates and rotate on planet angle
        save().translate(x, y).rotate(a)

        // set the line width and color in float HSL format
        lineWidth(5).stroke(.58, .5, .7)

        // atmosphere haze
        circle(0, 0, r)

        // draw the planet image
        image(res.planet, -r, -r, 2*r, 2*r)

        // MUST restore the drawing context
        restore()
    }
```

That is all you need to see a bouncing planet.

Open the _planet.mod_ folder in a command console and type:

```
jam play
```

_Collider.JAM_ is going to scan the folder for sources and resources, start the server at port 9999, and open [http://localhost:9999](http://localhost:9999) in the default web browser.

Functions _draw()_ and _evo(dt)_ are going
to be evaluated in the main game loop,
since they are part of the _lab_ node.

*Any object with _draw()_ and _evo()_ in the _lab_ is alive.*

Provided resources are loaded and mapped to the scene graph automatically, so you can easily access the planet image simply as _res.planet_ (given that the image file was named "planet.png" or "planet.jpeg" - the name will match the filename).
No _loads_ or _imports_ are needed here.


The source for this example is available
at <a href="https://github.com/invadium/bits.mix/blob/master/planet.mod/lab.js">bits.mix/planet.mod/lab.js</a>.

To run it, clone the _bits.mix_ repository:

    git clone https://github.com/invadium/bits.mix

Then, run _Collider.JAM_ inside the _planet.mod_ folder:

    cd bits.mix/planet.mod

    jam

And open the browser at
[http://localhost:9999](http://localhost:9999)

Explore other examples in _bits.mix_ and other [mixes](#.mixes).









